<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Kingdoms period</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Three Kingdoms</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three Kingdoms period was the era of Chinese history following the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a>, during which the states of Wei, Shu and Wu contended for supremacy in the third century CE. It was marked by prolonged warfare and became one of the most celebrated periods in Chinese literature and folklore. Accounts of the Han Dynasty place it at the end of the dynasty's decline, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a> note it in the context of the later northern frontier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period.jpg" alt="Three Kingdoms period" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ian Kiu · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saam_Gwok_262_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King Muryeong's tomb at Gongju, found unlooted in 1971, held an epitaph stone naming the king, a rare case in which a <b>Three Kingdoms</b> royal tomb can be identified with certainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Its ascendancy shaped the politics of the <b>Three Kingdoms</b>: Silla was for a time a near-protectorate, as the stele and a bronze vessel inscribed with Gwanggaeto's name found in a Silla tomb in Gyeongju attest, while Baekje's long rivalry with Goguryeo drove its alliances with the southern Chinese…</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Goryeo grew out of the collapse of Unified Silla in the late ninth century, when regional strongmen divided the peninsula into the Later <b>Three Kingdoms</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…in 189, the warlord Cao Cao controlled the last Han emperor from 196 CE onwards, and the dynasty ended in 220 CE when his son Cao Pi deposed Emperor Xian and declared the Wei dynasty, opening the <b>Three Kingdoms period</b> before China was briefly reunified under the Jin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The rebel leaders Gyeon Hwon and Gung Ye carved out Later Baekje (from 892, proclaimed in 900) and Later Goguryeo (901), later renamed Taebong, reducing Silla to the area around Gyeongju in the period known as the Later <b>Three Kingdoms</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He sent tribute to Cao Cao and later dealt with the rival <b>Three Kingdoms</b>, and in 231 he marched to Beidi in support of Zhuge Liang's northern campaign against Cao Wei.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Military</a></li></ul></section>
